\title{Monotone Forcing Dynamics and Local Triangle Structure\\in Claw-Free Cubic Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Zero forcing is a deterministic propagation process on a finite simple graph. Beginning with a colored vertex set, a colored vertex may force an uncolored neighbor precisely when that neighbor is its unique uncolored neighbor. We develop the finite-state foundations needed for extremal investigations of zero forcing in claw-free cubic graphs. A legal move adds exactly one vertex; reachability is monotone under inclusion and cardinality and is antisymmetric; and forcing certificates compose. We prove that the zero forcing number is attained, derive the bound $\Zf(G)\leq |V(G)|-1$ for graphs without isolated vertices, and show that equality holds for every nontrivial complete graph. Finally, every vertex in a claw-free cubic graph is shown to lie in a triangle, yielding the same general bound for this class. Algorithms for validating certificates, exact subset search, and finding local triangle witnesses are also described.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. Zero forcing starts with a set $S\subseteq V$ of colored vertices. A colored vertex with exactly one uncolored neighbor may force that neighbor to become colored. A set that can eventually color all vertices is a \emph{zero forcing set}; its minimum possible size is the \emph{zero forcing number} $\Zf(G)$.

The process translates dynamic propagation into static optimization. Its witnesses are explicit: an initial set and an ordered list of legal forces. Upper bounds arise from constructing such certificates, while lower bounds arise by proving that smaller sets encounter unavoidable ambiguity. The process is relevant whenever local uniqueness permits deterministic resolution, including network monitoring and graph-constrained elimination.

Our focus is the order structure of forcing and its first consequences for claw-free cubic graphs. A cubic graph has degree three at every vertex. A claw is an induced $K_{1,3}$: a center with three pairwise nonadjacent neighbors. Combining cubicity with claw-freeness forces every vertex into a triangle. This local fact is the first structural ingredient for more refined block decompositions.

\section{Definitions}
\begin{definition}
For $u\in V$, let
\[
\N_G(u)=\{v\in V:uv\in E\}
\]
be its open neighborhood. The graph is \emph{cubic} if $|\N_G(u)|=3$ for every $u\in V$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Given a colored set $S\subseteq V$, a pair $(u,w)$ is a \emph{legal force}, written $u\to w$, if
\[
u\in S,\quad w\notin S,\quad uw\in E,\quad \N_G(u)\setminus S=\{w\}.
\]
After the force, the colored set is $S\cup\{w\}$.
\end{definition}

A \emph{forcing sequence} is a finite sequence of legal forces. We write $S\forces T$ when such a sequence, possibly empty, starts at $S$ and ends at $T$.

\begin{definition}
A set $S$ is a \emph{zero forcing set} if $S\forces V$. The \emph{zero forcing number} is
\[
\Zf(G)=\min\{|S|:S\subseteq V\text{ and }S\forces V\}.
\]
The family is nonempty because $V\forces V$ by the empty sequence.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{claw} is an induced subgraph isomorphic to $K_{1,3}$. Thus a graph is \emph{claw-free} precisely when, for every vertex $v$ and three distinct neighbors $a,b,c$, at least one of $ab$, $ac$, or $bc$ is an edge.
\end{definition}

\section{Monotone forcing dynamics}
\begin{theorem}[Single-step growth]
If a legal force transforms $S$ into $T$, then $T=S\cup\{w\}$ for some $w\notin S$. Consequently,
\[
S\subseteq T\qquad\text{and}\qquad |T|=|S|+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A legal force colors one specified uncolored vertex and leaves all colored vertices colored. Adjoining an element not already in a finite set increases its cardinality by one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sequence monotonicity]
If $S\forces T$, then $S\subseteq T$ and $|S|\leq |T|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the length of the sequence. The empty sequence gives equality. For a nonempty sequence, combine the induction hypothesis with single-step inclusion for the final force. Cardinality is monotone under inclusion for finite sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Equal-cardinality rigidity]
If $S\forces T$ and $|S|=|T|$, then $S=T$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Sequence monotonicity gives $S\subseteq T$; finite sets of equal size related by inclusion are equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Antisymmetry]
If $S\forces T$ and $T\forces S$, then $S=T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Monotonicity gives $|S|\leq |T|$ and $|T|\leq |S|$. The cardinalities are equal, so equal-cardinality rigidity applies.
\end{proof}

Thus forcing reachability is reflexive, transitive, and antisymmetric: it is a partial order on colored states. Cardinality is a rank function, increasing by one at every genuine move. The directed state graph consequently has no nontrivial directed cycle, and a sequence starting at $S$ contains at most $|V|-|S|$ genuine forces.

\section{Composition and minimum certificates}
\begin{theorem}[Certificate composition]
If $S\forces T$ and $T$ is zero forcing, then $S$ is zero forcing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A certificate $T\forces V$ exists. Concatenating it with $S\forces T$ gives $S\forces V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]
For every finite simple graph $G$, some zero forcing set $S$ satisfies $|S|=\Zf(G)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The power set of $V$ is finite, and its subfamily of zero forcing sets is nonempty because it contains $V$. The finite nonempty collection of their cardinalities has a least member, attained by one of those sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Certificate upper bound]
If $S$ is zero forcing, then $\Zf(G)\leq |S|$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The zero forcing number is the minimum cardinality among all zero forcing sets.
\end{proof}

These facts make certificates modular. One may force an intermediate structured state and append a previously established certificate from that state.

\section{Co-singleton certificates}
\begin{theorem}[Co-singleton certificate]
If $w$ has a neighbor, then $V\setminus\{w\}$ is a zero forcing set.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $u$ be a neighbor of $w$. Initially $w$ is the only uncolored vertex. Hence it is the unique uncolored neighbor of $u$, and the force $u\to w$ colors the entire graph.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-isolation bound]
If $G$ is finite, nonempty, and has no isolated vertices, then
\[
\Zf(G)\leq |V|-1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Choose $w\in V$. It has a neighbor, so the co-singleton theorem gives a zero forcing set of cardinality $|V|-1$.
\end{proof}

An isolated vertex can never be forced and must be initially colored, so non-isolation is the natural condition for this particular construction.

\section{Complete graphs}
Let $K_n$ be the complete graph on $n$ vertices.

\begin{lemma}[Available forces in a complete graph]
If a legal force is available from $S$ in $K_n$, then $|S|=n-1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $u\to w$ is legal, every vertex other than $u$ is adjacent to $u$, while $w$ is its unique uncolored neighbor. Since $u$ is colored, every vertex except $w$ is colored.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Complete-graph lower bound]
Every zero forcing set $S$ of $K_n$ satisfies $|S|\geq n-1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $S=V$, the result is immediate. Otherwise a successful sequence has a first force, and the preceding lemma shows that its initial state contains $n-1$ vertices. Equivalently, with at least two uncolored vertices, every colored vertex sees at least two uncolored neighbors and cannot force.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete-graph formula]
For $n\geq2$,
\[
\Zf(K_n)=n-1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lower bound is the previous lemma. For the upper bound, leave one vertex uncolored. It has a neighbor because $n\geq2$, so the co-singleton theorem applies.
\end{proof}

Complete graphs show that greater edge density need not ease zero forcing: many adjacencies create ambiguity rather than unique targets.

\section{Claw-free cubic graphs}
\begin{theorem}[Local triangle theorem]
Every vertex of a claw-free cubic graph lies in a triangle. Explicitly, for each $v\in V$ there are $a,b\in V$ such that
\[
va,vb,ab\in E.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cubicity supplies three distinct neighbors $a,b,c$ of $v$. If they were pairwise nonadjacent, the subgraph induced by $\{v,a,b,c\}$ would be a claw centered at $v$. Claw-freeness therefore makes at least one pair adjacent. That pair and $v$ form a triangle.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Elementary claw-free cubic bound]
If $G$ is a finite nonempty claw-free cubic graph, then
\[
\Zf(G)\leq |V|-1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The local triangle theorem gives every vertex a neighbor. Apply the non-isolation bound.
\end{proof}

The numerical argument uses only non-isolation, but the triangle witness reveals substantially more structure. It suggests treating these graphs as assemblies of triangles and diamonds rather than arbitrary degree-three networks.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Certificate validation}
Given $G$, an initial set $S$, and proposed forces $(u_i,w_i)$, maintain a colored set $C$, initially $S$. For each pair, verify
\[
u_i\in C,\quad w_i\notin C,\quad u_iw_i\in E,\quad \N_G(u_i)\setminus C=\{w_i\}.
\]
If the conditions hold, insert $w_i$; otherwise reject. Accept a zero forcing certificate exactly when the final set is $V$. With adjacency lists, the cost is
\[
O\!\left(\sum_i\deg(u_i)\right)\subseteq O(|V|\Delta).
\]
For cubic graphs this is linear in $|V|$.

\subsection{Propagation and exact computation}
To propagate from $S$, repeatedly choose a colored vertex having exactly one uncolored neighbor and perform the force. Stop successfully when every vertex is colored, or unsuccessfully when a complete scan finds no move. A direct rescanning implementation is $O(|V||E|)$; queue-based updates avoid much redundant work.

To compute $\Zf(G)$ exactly, enumerate subsets in nondecreasing cardinality and propagate each candidate. The first successful cardinality is $\Zf(G)$. The worst case examines $2^{|V|}$ subsets, although symmetry and certificate caching may reduce the search.

\subsection{Triangle witnesses}
At each vertex of a cubic graph, inspect the three pairs among its three neighbors. In a claw-free graph, the local triangle theorem guarantees that at least one pair is adjacent. With adjacency-set queries this requires constant work per vertex and $O(|V|)$ total work.

\section{Examples and applications}
For the path $P_n$ with $n\geq2$, one endpoint initiates a chain of unique-neighbor forces, so $\Zf(P_n)=1$. For a cycle $C_n$, two adjacent vertices force around the cycle, while one colored vertex has two uncolored neighbors; hence $\Zf(C_n)=2$. At the opposite extreme, $\Zf(K_n)=n-1$.

The triangular prism is a claw-free cubic graph on six vertices. Every vertex lies in one of its triangular faces. The general result gives $\Zf(G)\leq5$, though graph-specific certificates do better. Such gaps motivate bounds that exploit the global arrangement of triangle blocks.

In network language, each force is a step without local ambiguity. In graph-constrained elimination, a unique unresolved neighbor enables deterministic resolution. Monotonicity and certificate composition support reusable, independently checkable propagation plans.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The forcing relation is ranked, monotone, acyclic, and compositional. These facts support dynamic programming and explain the form of upper-bound arguments. For claw-free cubic graphs, a forbidden local configuration yields a positive one: every neighborhood contains an edge, so every vertex belongs to a triangle.

The next objective is a global triangle-and-diamond decomposition for connected claw-free cubic graphs. This should be combined with the independence number $\alpha(G)$ and with contraction multigraphs, where parallel edges require an explicitly multigraph-based treatment. Hamiltonian cycles in such contractions are expected to support certificates for the division-free inequality
\[
2\Zf(G)\leq T+2D+4,
\]
where $T$ and $D$ count triangle and diamond blocks. A further objective is the characterization of exceptional families satisfying
\[
\Zf(G)=\alpha(G)+1.
\]
Exhaustive generation of small claw-free cubic graphs can test intermediate decomposition statements and candidate certificates.

\section{Conclusion}
A legal zero-forcing move adds exactly one vertex, and finite forcing sequences grow monotonically. Equal-sized reachable states coincide, mutual reachability forces equality, and certificates concatenate. Minimum certificates exist; a co-singleton certificate gives $\Zf(G)\leq|V|-1$ without isolated vertices; and complete graphs attain this bound. In claw-free cubic graphs every vertex lies in a triangle, yielding the same universal estimate while exposing the clustered local geometry needed for sharper global results.

\end{document}

